  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "This storage-only consumer must delegate load/store using node_path\n without resolving contents. Domain requires the callback, but invoking\n this guard would violate the no-content-read invariant of the test.\n Other graph fixtures exercise content resolution."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` cite on `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/storage.rs#impl[StoredGraph/Domain]/fn[node_content]` is a valid coverage exception.\n\n- **Placement:** It is on an explicit function with a body. The explanation is in fenced citation commentary, which the built-in text accepts.\n- **Substance:** The stated reason is a real invariant, not just difficulty. `StoredGraph` is a storage-only fixture, and its `load_evidence` and `store_evidence` delegate to `FileEvidenceStorage` using only `node_path`. `node_content` is a guard that panics with \"storage operations must not read graph contents\". It is never meant to run, so zero execution is the intended outcome.\n- **Agreement with source:**\n  - `Domain` does require the callback, because `node_content` is declared in `trait[Domain]` in `src/domain.rs`.\n  - `StoredGraph` uses `UnreadContents`, an uninhabited enum, as its `NodeContent` type.\n  - The tests that use the fixture are `malformed_records_are_storage_errors_and_bad_outputs_are_output_errors` and `consumer_delegates_evidence_storage_using_only_the_requested_nodes_path`. `StoredReview` never touches contents, and the other test calls only `load_evidence`, `store_evidence` and `node_path`. So the commentary matches the current code.\n- **Other fixtures:** Other fixtures do implement `node_content` and exercise content resolution. Examples are `Memory/Domain` in `src/agent/tests.rs` and `src/graph/validation_tests.rs`, and `Application/Domain` in `tests/contracts.rs`.\n- **Redundancy:** The item has no `{impl}` or `{test}` reference to the same target, so the cite is not redundant.",
